WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.012 --> 00:00:08.291
[NOISE] It's not really about what happens
at the end of the race,

00:00:08.291 --> 00:00:15.514
it's really having the courage to show up
and to start.

00:00:15.514 --> 00:00:21.768
And to look at something that seems really
impossible, but to break it down and

00:00:21.768 --> 00:00:27.759
not only do it, but also enjoy it and find
a new part of what I'm capable of.

00:00:27.759 --> 00:00:33.164
[MUSIC]

00:00:33.164 --> 00:00:36.292
I worked at a hedge fund on Wall Street
for

00:00:36.292 --> 00:00:42.590
five years and I realized at one point
that I really needed an outlet.

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:45.370
I felt like I was in a job where,

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:50.140
you know my passion really started in the
morning when I was out riding my bike or

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:53.670
running or talking to people about it
later in the day.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:01:00.520
For me, my definition of balance, is that
there really isn't balance.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:05.331
Because I like to be 100% driving toward
what I find most fulfilling, and

00:01:05.331 --> 00:01:07.932
where I feel like I can make a difference.

00:01:07.932 --> 00:01:10.868
[MUSIC]

00:01:10.868 --> 00:01:14.603
I am the Women's Product Manager at
Specialized, so

00:01:14.603 --> 00:01:18.760
my mission is to bring more women into the
sport of cycling.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.490
At the core I'm responsible for

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:27.970
really managing our women's product lines,
all designed to fit women and

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:31.900
give women the most enjoyable experience
possible out cycling.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:37.300
When I meet women who are curious about
cycling,

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:42.180
one of my biggest tasks is just to really
spark that curiosity, and

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:46.190
to bring them into a sport that has really
defined my life in so many ways.

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:49.507
And is a passion that I love every single
day.

00:01:49.507 --> 00:01:54.745
[MUSIC]

00:01:54.745 --> 00:01:57.690
I was the youngest woman to run a marathon
on all seven continents.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:04.960
So that included 26.2 miles across the
Gold Coast in Australia Florida

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:11.210
in North America, Brazil in South America,
Dublin in Europe, Mount Kilimanjaro in

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:16.350
Africa, the Beijing Marathon in Asia, and
then of course, the pinnacle experience,

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:20.460
which was the Antarctica Marathon, way
down at the bottom of the earth.

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:26.330
Ultimately, I think, when I finally got
the record, it had been a quest for

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:27.300
over two years.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:31.120
And I was so excited about it, and so
passionate about it.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:34.760
And I remember getting the certificate,
and being like, oh, that's nice,

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:39.550
and what I was really excited about were
the people that I met along the way,

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:41.860
the sort of memories I had of these
places.

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:46.070
And, it just really opened up my world
view in a way where,

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:50.690
when I got to the end of that road, the
record was a nice piece of paper, but

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:56.200
the process was really where a lot of the
learning happened.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.670
I thought that was one definition of
challenge.

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:02.990
And, the Ironman opened up a whole new
world.

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:07.860
And Ironman is a journey that takes you
from a lake

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:11.450
to a bike to a run and every high and low
in between.

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:15.120
And there is something in the suffering of
Ironman, and

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:18.080
the communal suffering, that's really
pushed me forward.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:19.690
It's been a goal that I trained for

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:23.120
and it's also been something that's
defined my life.

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:25.550
It's a way that I like to live.

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:29.160
It's more then a race, it's an experience
and a lifestyle for me.

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:36.360
I don't think I could do it unless I
really enjoyed the process.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.980
Hello, greetings from the top of the Blue
Nile Course.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:43.050
One of the things that I ask myself a lot
is, what's next?

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:48.733
I decided to apply to Stanford as a way to
transition into

00:03:48.733 --> 00:03:54.050
a fitness-focused career that was based
more around my core passions.

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:56.590
I had four months before I had to be at
school.

00:03:56.590 --> 00:03:59.500
and what better way to fill four months
than to

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:03.080
ride a bicycle from Cairo to Cape Town?

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:06.330
So I entered the longest bicycle stage
race in the world.

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:10.280
I left a great job, great apartment, great
life in New York City,

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:14.610
got on a plane to Cairo, almost walked out
and

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:17.870
asked the pilot to turn the plane around,
and I didn't wanna go.

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:20.439
But as the plane was descending into
Cairo,

00:04:20.439 --> 00:04:24.061
I realized that I was embarking on
something really special.

00:04:24.061 --> 00:04:28.266
[MUSIC]

00:04:28.266 --> 00:04:32.980
I took a different route out of the GSB
than many of my classmates.

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:35.780
My opportunity at Specialized was very
unexpected.

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:41.380
I came into this wonderful place with all
these bikes and people who loved to bike

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:45.610
and after five years in finance, it's been
just a really exciting new challenge to

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:49.730
work on products, and to work on products
that I'm personally very passionate about.

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:53.910
&gt;&gt; What's great for us is people that are
passionate about cycling.

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:54.610
Right?

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:56.898
Not just they like to bike, they love to
bike.

00:04:56.898 --> 00:05:02.290
[LAUGH] And know how much of a life
changer it can be for people.

00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:06.732
So, when we met Erin, that passion for
cycling, but

00:05:06.732 --> 00:05:10.782
more importantly her drive and
determination,

00:05:10.782 --> 00:05:17.515
in everything that she had done in her
life, it was like wow, she's fantastic.

00:05:17.515 --> 00:05:23.620
&gt;&gt; I don't think I could do my current job
without the two years I spent at the GSB.

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:26.610
Coming into an environment where feedback
is very direct,

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:29.100
it comes from all over the world.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:34.725
I feel like the GSB empowered me to
prioritize my time to listen

00:05:34.725 --> 00:05:40.905
to feedback and to really have the ability
to step into leadership.

00:05:40.905 --> 00:05:44.150
I think I've followed a very unusual path.

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:52.470
I think it's just a part of my philosophy
which is always asking, what's next?

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:56.750
And always striving toward whatever is
around the next bend.

00:05:56.750 --> 00:06:00.892
And not being afraid to step into
something very uncomfortable.

00:06:00.892 --> 00:06:02.460
[MUSIC]

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:06.910
For me it's the next Ironman, the next
bike ride, it's just sort of

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:11.235
moving forward, constantly, and in every
possible direction.

00:06:11.235 --> 00:06:21.235
[MUSIC]

